# Backend Rules

Applies to API routes, Server Actions, middleware, database, and auth. Do not put UI markup here.

## Scope

| In scope | Out of scope |
|----------|--------------|
| `src/app/api/**` | React components in `src/components/` |
| `src/lib/server/**` | Page layout and styling |
| `middleware.ts` | Client-side form validation only |
| `prisma/**`, migrations | Marketing copy in route handlers |

## API route conventions

- One concern per route file under `src/app/api/[resource]/route.ts`
- Export named handlers: `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, `DELETE`
- Return `NextResponse.json()` with consistent shape:

```ts
// Success
return NextResponse.json({ data }, { status: 200 });

// Error
return NextResponse.json({ error: "Message" }, { status: 400 });
```

- Validate input with Zod before any DB or external call
- Never expose stack traces or internal errors to clients

## Security

- Read secrets from env only (`process.env.*`); never commit `.env`
- Rate-limit public endpoints (contact form, auth) when implemented
- Sanitize user input; use parameterized queries (Prisma) — no raw string SQL
- Set HTTP-only cookies for sessions; never store tokens in localStorage from server design

## Auth (when implemented)

- Follow checklist in `.cursor/plans/auth-implementation.md`
- Protect routes via `middleware.ts` matcher
- Hash passwords with bcrypt/argon2; never log credentials

## Database

- Schema changes via Prisma migrations only
- Use transactions for multi-step writes
- Log errors server-side; return generic messages to client

## Contact form (Phase 1 pattern)

```ts
// POST /api/contact — validate, send email, return 200/400
// Use CONTACT from @/lib/constants for recipient
```

## Documentation

When adding or changing API routes, run the **document-api** skill (`.cursor/skills/document-api/skill.md`) and update route docs.

## Do not

- Return unverified financial or credential claims from API responses
- Call external APIs without timeout and error handling
- Mix JSX rendering into route handlers
